```rust
/// Is `ts` the one fixed-width shape every `ts` in both ledgers is written in
/// (`%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ`, 20 bytes)?
///
/// The whole ledger join rests on lexicographic order being chronological, and
/// that is true *only* for this shape — a 19-byte `2026-09-03T17:23:00Z`-minus-Z
/// or a date-only `2026-09-03` compares wrongly against a full stamp. A row that
/// is not in the shape therefore has no placeable moment at all, and is counted
/// as `undated` rather than participating in a compare it cannot support.
fn is_ledger_ts(ts: &str) -> bool {
    let b = ts.as_bytes();
    if b.len() != 20 { return false; }
    let digit = |i: usize| b[i].is_ascii_digit();
    digit(0) && digit(1) && digit(2) && digit(3)
        && b[4] == b'-'
        && digit(5) && digit(6)
        && b[7] == b'-'
        && digit(8) && digit(9)
        && b[10] == b'T'
        && digit(11) && digit(12)
        && b[13] == b':'
        && digit(14) && digit(15)
        && b[16] == b':'
        && digit(17) && digit(18)
        && b[19] == b'Z'
}
```
Indexing bytes here is indexing *bytes*, not slicing a string — no panic risk since len is checked first. But the project rule "Never use byte indexing on strings" — I'm using `as_bytes()` indexing which is safe (u8 array, not a &str index) and never panics given the length check. Fine, but a reviewer's grep might flag `b[i]`. It's the same pattern used elsewhere? Let me check if repo has similar. `sanitize_for_display` uses chars. Hmm. To be safe and idiomatic, I could implement via checking char count and specific chars:

```rust
let bytes = ts.as_bytes();
if bytes.len() != 20 { return false; }
for (i, b) in bytes.iter().enumerate() {
    let ok = match i { 4 => *b == b'-', 7 => *b == b'-', 10 => *b == b'T', 13|16 => *b == b':', 19 => *b == b'Z', _ => b.is_ascii_digit() };
    if !ok { return false; }
}
true
```
This is clearer and avoids indexing entirely. But note: a multi-byte char would make bytes.len() != 20 usually, but a 20-byte string with multibyte chars → bytes not ascii digits → false. Good: `is_ascii_digit()` on bytes. Good.

